{
 "title": "Controlling Untrusted AI: A Survey",
 "thesis": "Deployed AI agents must meet the requirements their deployer places on them, and they cannot be trusted to do so on their own. On the attack side the survey distinguishes three tiers: benign failures (the agent errs and no one is attacking), external attacks (an outsider redirects the agent through what it reads, calls, or remembers), and insider threats (a person with legitimate partial access, or the model itself pursuing objectives of its own). On the defence side it separates measures inside the executing model (training, steering) from measures outside it (constraints, monitoring, intervention), with verification testing either; its position is that guarantees come from outside the model, while measures inside it lower the base rate the outside layer must catch. For every mechanism the survey records the threat model and assumptions under which it holds, in particular whether it survives an agent or insider that actively tries to defeat it, how its effectiveness depends on the attacker's capabilities and incentives, what it costs in safety and usefulness, and what evidence supports it. The adversarial agent is the stress test, not the premise.",
 "readers": [
  "Researchers inside one mechanism family (agent security: injection defences, isolation, information-flow control; guardrails; monitoring; control evaluation; formal enforcement; post-training; steering) who want the other families on one map with the same assessment questions answered.",
  "Teams deploying agents with real credentials who must choose and combine mechanisms for a requirement and a threat model.",
  "Evaluators, auditors, and policy staff who need to know what evidence a control claim rests on and which mechanisms satisfy which governance requirements (human oversight, logging, risk management, robustness)."
 ],
